# Example B — two papers in the same field, cross-paper defeat and the `contested` flip
#
# Purpose: show that "paper + reviews" and "corpus of papers" are NOT a different calculus — they
# are the SAME Dung framework at a larger population size (spec §8, cross-framework
# non-monotonicity). Here two independent papers formalize contrary conclusions about the same
# (M, accuracy, D). Because both land on the SAME atoms under `≡` (spec §3.2), their conclusions
# are a declared `contrary` pair and each rebuts the other — a 2-cycle that grounded semantics
# labels `undec`, so the claim is `contested`, not silently "both justified".
#
# This example is also the atom-matching stress test (the real cross-corpus risk): if paper_B's
# claim formalized to `improves(M, acc, D)` with a DIFFERENT accuracy atom or a different D, the
# contrary pair would not match and NO attack would form — a silent false-independence. The
# `binding` audit (assumption A3) is where that risk is caught; it is annotated below.

artifact corpus_field_X at sha256:bbbb...
policy empirical-v1
use backends [nd@1]

# --- Paper A's positive claim ------------------------------------------------------------------

claim c_pos
  nl      = "Method M improves accuracy on distribution D"
  formal  = improves(M, accuracy, D)
  binding = { author = alice, audit-status = reviewed,
              rationale = "same (M,accuracy,D) atoms as c_neg; verified identical under nf" }

leaf a_e1 : reports(paperA_exp1, effect(M, accuracy, D, positive))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [paperA/table_2.csv#row=mean]

leaf a_e2 : randomized(paperA_exp1)
  kind = attested
  provenance = user
  refs = [paperA.pdf#sec=4]

leaf a_e3 : powered(paperA_exp1)
  kind = attested
  provenance = user
  refs = [paperA.pdf#sec=4]

leaf a_e4 : generalizes(M, accuracy, D)
  kind = attested
  provenance = user
  refs = [paperA.pdf#sec=5]

arg pa : supports(c_pos) by controlled_experiment(M, accuracy, D, paperA_exp1)
  discharge randomization     with a_e2
  discharge adequate_power    with a_e3
  discharge external_validity with a_e4

# --- Paper B's contradicting null result -------------------------------------------------------

claim c_neg
  nl      = "Method M does not improve accuracy on distribution D"
  formal  = not_improves(M, accuracy, D)
  binding = { author = bob, audit-status = reviewed,
              rationale = "failed replication of paperA; SAME M, accuracy metric, and D" }

leaf b_e1 : reports(paperB_exp1, effect(M, accuracy, D, negligible))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [paperB/table_1.csv#row=mean]

leaf b_e2 : randomized(paperB_exp1)
  kind = attested
  provenance = user
  refs = [paperB.pdf#sec=3]

leaf b_e3 : powered(paperB_exp1)
  kind = attested
  provenance = user
  refs = [paperB.pdf#sec=3]

arg pb : supports(c_neg) by null_result(M, accuracy, D, paperB_exp1)
  discharge randomization  with b_e2
  discharge adequate_power with b_e3

# --- The cross-paper conflict ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Mutual rebuttal: pa concludes improves(...), pb concludes not_improves(...), and both
# orientations are declared contrary in empirical-v1. Neither paper is preferred (v0.1 has no
# preference ordering wired in yet), so neither defeat is one-sided: a 2-cycle.
rebut pb pa    # paper B rebuts paper A's positive claim
rebut pa pb    # paper A rebuts paper B's null claim

status c_pos
status c_neg

# ==============================================================================================
# EXPECTED VERDICT (golden oracle)
# ==============================================================================================
# Compiled AF: two arguments, mutually attacking (a 2-cycle):
#   pa <-> pb
# Grounded labelling: neither is `in`, neither is `out` -> both `undec` (spec §8, cycles yield
# undec, assumption A5).
# Claim status (spec §8 priority):
#   c_pos -> contested   (its support pa is undec, none is in)
#   c_neg -> contested   (its support pb is undec, none is in)
#
# Teaching points:
#  1. No new calculus was needed to go from one paper to two — same AF, one more argument and a
#     pair of edges. Reviews scale in identically: a reviewer's rebuttal is just another support
#     term + attack compiled into this graph.
#  2. `contested` (not "both justified") is the epistemically honest answer to an unresolved
#     field disagreement, and it fell out of grounded semantics, not a hand-coded rule.
#  3. The attack ONLY formed because both claims normalize to the same atoms. If a corpus-scale
#     deployment wants preferences (e.g. larger-N replication wins) instead of a symmetric
#     2-cycle, that is the spec §8.1 flip criterion / a preference ordering — a known extension
#     point, recorded, not an ad hoc patch.
